problems.problem:
A natural number $n$, $n \ge 2$, is called "friendly" if there exists a family of nonempty subsets $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_n$ of the set $\{1, 2, \dots, n\}$ such that:

1) $i \notin A_i$ for every $i = \overline{1, n}$;

2) $i \in A_j$ if and only if $j \notin A_i$, for all distinct $i, j \in \{1, \dots, n\}$;

3) $A_i \cap A_j \ne \emptyset$ for all $i, j \in \{1, \dots, n\}$.

Prove that:

a) the number $7$ is friendly;

b) $n$ is friendly if and only if $n \ge 7$.

Let $p, q \in \mathbb{N}^*$, $p \ge 2$, $q \ge 2$. We say that a set $X$ has property (S) if, however we choose $p$ subsets $B_i \subset X$, $i = \overline{1, p}$, not necessarily distinct, each with $q$ elements, there exists a subset $Y \subset X$ with $p$ elements such that the intersection of $Y$ with each $B_i$, $i = \overline{1, p}$, has at most one element. Prove that:

a) for $p = 4$ and $q = 3$, no set with $9$ elements has property (S);

b) no set $X$ with $pq - q$ elements has property (S);

c) every set $X$ with $pq - q + 1$ elements has property (S).
